 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 10,
   "text": "EYRE SQUARE. Eight acres of green in the middle of Galway, given to the city in 1710 and now officially the John F. Kennedy Memorial Park. Kennedy visited in 1963 and the town's never stopped telling people."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 10,
   "text": "BROWNE DOORWAY. A carved stone doorway of 1627 from a merchant's house. It's one of the Fourteen Tribes of Galway, and it's been rehomed in a park, which is a very Galway thing to do."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 15,
   "text": "GALWAY CATHEDRAL. Finished in 1965 on the site of the old city jail. A big stone dome, a lot of Connemara marble and a rose window you could lose a day in."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 33,
   "text": "THE CLADDAGH. A fishing village of thatched cottages outside the old walls, with its own king, who was the fishermen's chosen mayor. It's also the home of the Claddagh ring: two hands holding a heart under a crown, for love, loyalty and friendship."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 39,
   "text": "THE SPANISH ARCH. Built in 1584 as part of the city walls to guard the quay where Spanish merchants unloaded wine. Galway's been trading with Spain for ages. The arch now guards a lot of ice-cream."
  },
  {
   "x": 61,
   "y": 35,
   "text": "GALWAY DOCKS. A working harbour since the Middle Ages. The Galway hooker, a sturdy black-hulled sailing boat with red-brown sails, still races in Cruinniu na mBad in summer. They're slower than the gossip."
  }
 ],
